<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Yique</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">293 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Yique was fought in 293 BCE near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> and the allied armies of Han and Wei. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bai-qi">Bai Qi</a> won a crushing victory; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> claims 240,000 enemy dead. The battle broke the power of Han and Wei to resist Qin and opened the way for its further expansion in the central plain.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By 294 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> under King Zhaoxiang had become a major military power, and its conquests began with its neighbours Wei and Han. The two states had long been enemies and paid little attention to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bai-qi">Bai Qi</a> attacked Han and took an important fortress. Alarmed, Wei and Han joined forces and gathered 240,000 troops across a wide front of five fortresses, cities and defensive positions along rivers and mountain ranges. Bai Qi had only 120,000 men, but the allies feared Qin's better trained and equipped troops and stayed on the defensive, and the campaign stood in stalemate until 293 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Seeing that Wei and Han were still hostile to each other, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bai-qi">Bai Qi</a> chose to divide and conquer. He scouted the allied lines for weaknesses, drew the attention of the main Han forces with small ambushes and threw the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> army against the weakly held Wei positions. Wei officers believed Han had failed them on purpose, and Han, to preserve its own forces, stopped supporting its ally. Over the following months Bai Qi took the Wei positions one by one without having to fight the Han army.</p><p>He then turned on the Han troops, who were trapped and tried to break out, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> cavalry let none escape. The allied general Gongsun Xi was captured, and five Han and Wei cities, Yique among them, fell to Qin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The battle brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> influence into central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for the first time. With their armies destroyed, Wei and Han ceded land to Qin in return for a temporary peace, but their end was only delayed: Qin conquered Han in 230 BCE and Wei in 225 BCE. The site of the battle, Yique, is the place now known as Longmen, in the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Yique” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bai Qi defeated Han and Wei at <b>Yique</b> in 293 BCE and captured Ying, the capital of Chu, in 278 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Military</a></li></ul></section>
